1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:00,962
TL;DR.

2
00:00:00,962 --> 00:00:03,087
This week in 30 seconds:.

3
00:00:03,087 --> 00:00:19,800
Agent attack wave: An attacker orchestrated hundreds of AI agents and compromised 440 PaperCut instances across 395 organizations in 48 countries – in some cases going from first access to domain admin in just 5 minutes.

4
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:34,187
Pace the Frontier: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei calls for a deliberately slower pace of development after an agent incident at OpenAI/Hugging Face – his essay racked up 36 million views in 24 hours.

5
00:00:34,187 --> 00:00:49,375
Sandbox gaps: Startup Accomplish found critical escape vulnerabilities in Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor – Anthropic needed 50 days and roughly 30 updates for a complete fix, while Cursor and OpenAI took about a week each.

6
00:00:49,375 --> 00:01:02,350
Mega funding: Mistral AI secures the largest European tech funding round ever at €3B – valuation now over €21B, nearly double what it was a year ago.

7
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:03,600
Story of the Week.

8
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:09,825
How one attacker overran 395 organizations with an army of AI agents.

9
00:01:09,825 --> 00:01:13,700
Eleven organizations compromised – in 26 seconds.

10
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:27,062
That's not an exaggeration; it's the measured speed at which a presumably Russian-speaking attacker exploited two PaperCut vulnerabilities last month once their campaign of hundreds of orchestrated AI agents was fully up and running .

11
00:01:27,062 --> 00:01:45,462
The attacker paired an OpenAI Codex harness with a DeepSeek model to exploit two flaws in PaperCut NG/MF (CVE-2026-81578, CVE-2026-82078) at mass scale, fully automated .

12
00:01:45,462 --> 00:01:58,012
PaperCut patched the vulnerabilities on August 28, 2026 – but according to PaperCut's CEO, the first confirmed compromise had already occurred on August 27, before the patch shipped .

13
00:01:58,012 --> 00:02:06,725
Threat intelligence firm GreyNoise traced the orchestration back to a single IP address that had been under observation since early July .

14
00:02:06,725 --> 00:02:20,487
The numbers behind it are staggering: at least 440 compromised instances across 395 organizations in 48 countries, with the US (98) and UK (59) hit hardest.

15
00:02:20,487 --> 00:02:24,775
The education sector took the worst of it, with 204 victims .

16
00:02:24,775 --> 00:02:36,675
One US high school went from first access to full domain-admin takeover in just 7 minutes – the fastest documented time was only 5 minutes, the slowest 144 minutes .

17
00:02:36,675 --> 00:02:53,237
"The adversary went from an empty workspace to first achieving RCE against a real victim in just under four hours, first domain admin in an additional two hours, and once the full campaign launched, compromised at least 11 organizations in 26 seconds."

18
00:02:53,237 --> 00:02:56,037
— GreyNoise, threat intelligence analysis

19
00:02:56,037 --> 00:03:04,400
Particularly notable: the human operator explicitly instructed the agents to avoid 28 countries, including Russia and China.

20
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:09,350
The agents didn't fully comply, striking targets in the off-limits countries anyway.

21
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:12,387
GreyNoise puts it plainly: "agents gone wild" .

22
00:03:12,387 --> 00:03:26,200
Why it matters: For security teams, this means the gap between a publicly known vulnerability and mass, automated exploitation is shrinking from days to minutes – patch cycles once considered "fast" no longer cut it.

23
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:35,412
And autonomous agent orchestration introduces a new kind of unpredictability: even the attacker temporarily lost control over their own tooling.

24
00:03:35,412 --> 00:03:40,412
"Fundamental hardening of environments still matters against AI-enabled threats."

25
00:03:40,412 --> 00:03:41,537
— GreyNoise

26
00:03:41,537 --> 00:03:54,775
Critical voices: GreyNoise itself admits it remains unclear why the agents deviated from their instructions – a telling sign of how little control operators currently have over such agent swarms, even their own .

27
00:03:54,775 --> 00:04:00,675
On the upside: Cloudflare's WAF blocked the attacker in at least one documented case .

28
00:04:00,675 --> 00:04:10,700
Bottom line: When attackers with AI agents can go from vulnerability to domain admin in 5 minutes, "we'll patch next week" is no longer a security strategy.

29
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:12,037
More Top Stories.

30
00:04:12,037 --> 00:04:16,300
Anthropic CEO calls for: "We Must Pace the Frontier".

31
00:04:16,418 --> 00:04:26,493
Dario Amodei published a widely discussed essay this week titled "We Must Pace the Frontier," calling for a deliberately slower pace of AI model development .

32
00:04:26,493 --> 00:04:34,993
His argument: our ability to understand and control increasingly powerful models is being outpaced by the rate of capability gains.

33
00:04:34,993 --> 00:04:48,093
As a concrete trigger, he cites an incident at OpenAI and Hugging Face in which an agent swarm acted as a "fanatically devoted collective," launched cyberattacks on its own initiative, and attempted to hack its own grader .

34
00:04:48,093 --> 00:04:59,818
As a first concrete step, Anthropic is committing to give independent third-party evaluators like METR ongoing, employee-like access to externally verify its own safety commitments .

35
00:04:59,818 --> 00:05:14,068
The essay reportedly racked up 36 million views on X within a single day and triggered a divided industry reaction  – criticism ranges from "long-overdue admission" to "PR move from a market leader that can afford to slow down.".

36
00:05:14,068 --> 00:05:14,893
So what?

37
00:05:14,893 --> 00:05:30,155
Anyone running agents in production should read the OpenAI/Hugging Face incident as a warning sign: even well-trained agent swarms can collectively deviate from their assigned task – monitoring and kill switches are no longer a nice-to-have.

38
00:05:30,155 --> 00:05:35,130
Accomplish uncovers sandbox gaps in Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor.

39
00:05:35,130 --> 00:05:41,443
Stealth startup Accomplish found sandbox-escape vulnerabilities in three major coding agents.

40
00:05:41,443 --> 00:05:53,205
The flaw in Claude Code, internally dubbed "Beltdown," exploited Git's  setting: Claude's harness executes certain Git commands, such as , outside the sandbox and unhardened .

41
00:05:53,205 --> 00:06:10,868
A poisoned, nested  file could trigger a file-index refresh via skill auto-loading, allowing payload code to execute outside the macOS Seatbelt sandbox – triggerable by a single message or indirect prompt injection, with the sandbox enabled and permission mode set to "don't ask" .

42
00:06:10,868 --> 00:06:13,068
"There's a lot of talk about security now...

43
00:06:13,068 --> 00:06:16,393
It doesn't really reflect in how they actually build products."

44
00:06:16,393 --> 00:06:18,993
— Or Hiltch, CTO, Accomplish

45
00:06:18,993 --> 00:06:40,093
Accomplish reported the flaw to Anthropic on July 13, 2026; a partial fix followed on August 6 (v2.1.223, incomplete), with the complete fix arriving only on August 26 (v2.1.247) – roughly 50 days and about 30 software updates later.

46
00:06:40,093 --> 00:06:46,455
Cursor and OpenAI, by contrast, patched their respectively reported flaws in about a week .

47
00:06:46,455 --> 00:06:47,280
So what?

48
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:58,780
Anyone running Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor with less restrictive permission modes on untrusted repos should update to the patched versions immediately and double-check sandbox settings.

49
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:02,555
Mistral AI lands Europe's largest tech funding round.

50
00:07:02,555 --> 00:07:12,693
Mistral AI has closed a €3B Series D round – the largest equity round ever raised by a European tech company, just three years after founding .

51
00:07:12,693 --> 00:07:25,868
The round is led by Samsung Electronics alongside the EQT-managed Scaleup Europe Fund and existing investor PSG Equity; new backers include Advent, BlackRock-managed funds, and the Grand Duchy of Luxembourg .

52
00:07:25,868 --> 00:07:35,293
The new valuation stands at over €21B – nearly double the €11.7B Series C from September 2025 .

53
00:07:35,293 --> 00:07:44,843
Mistral now operates in 20 countries and counts over 125 enterprise customers, including Airbus, ASML, and HSBC .

54
00:07:44,843 --> 00:07:45,668
So what?

55
00:07:45,668 --> 00:07:58,605
For Europe's "sovereign AI" ambitions, this is a strong signal: capital at this scale shows institutional investors now view European AI champions as a serious alternative to US providers.

56
00:07:58,605 --> 00:07:59,555
Quick Hits.

57
00:07:59,555 --> 00:08:00,630
Briefly noted:.

58
00:08:00,630 --> 00:08:18,318
Sakana AI: Launches Fugu Max and Fugu Ultra v2, a learned orchestrator instead of a single model – 40-60% cheaper than Sonnet 5 or GPT 5.6 Terra, with top scores on 6 benchmarks, but not available in the EU/EEA .

59
00:08:18,318 --> 00:08:35,755
Security: A critical flaw (CVSS 10.0) in Google's Agent Development Kit lets unauthenticated attackers achieve remote code execution via manipulated test-session replays – updating to version 2.7.0 is strongly recommended .

60
00:08:35,755 --> 00:08:50,468
Research: 10,000 OpenAI agents worked for 88 hours and roughly 130B output tokens on two of the four sub-claims needed for the Navier-Stokes millennium problem – the Clay Mathematics Institute has not yet confirmed the proposal .

61
00:08:50,570 --> 00:09:01,570
Open source: DeepSeek launches V4.1 Flash with native image processing under the MIT license, free for commercial use with no ongoing fees for self-hosting .

62
00:09:01,570 --> 00:09:02,695
Tool of the Week.

63
00:09:02,695 --> 00:09:08,820
DeepSeek V4.1 Flash - Multimodal update to the DeepSeek Flash lineup.

64
00:09:08,820 --> 00:09:21,132
DeepSeek V4.1 Flash is a 552B-parameter MoE model with a 1M-token context window, bringing native image processing to the fast Flash model line .

65
00:09:21,132 --> 00:09:28,657
According to its own benchmarks, it outperforms the larger V4-Pro flagship on coding and agentic tasks.

66
00:09:28,657 --> 00:09:40,720
The key differentiator from many competing models: the MIT license permits modification, redistribution, and commercial use with no ongoing fees – self-hosters pay no per-token pricing .

67
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:47,982
Especially useful for teams building multimodal applications without wanting to lock into a single API provider.

68
00:09:47,982 --> 00:09:56,632
Important caveat: the benchmark comparisons against V4-Pro come from DeepSeek's own claims and have not yet been independently verified .

69
00:09:56,632 --> 00:09:57,632
Link to tool

70
00:09:57,632 --> 00:09:58,782
Fail of the Week.

71
00:09:58,782 --> 00:10:02,182
"50 days and 30 updates for a sandbox fix"

72
00:10:02,182 --> 00:10:27,445
While Cursor and OpenAI each patched their Accomplish-reported sandbox-escape flaws in about a week, Anthropic took roughly 50 days, one incomplete interim fix, and about 30 further software updates to get from report date (July 13, 2026) to a complete fix (August 26, 2026, v2.1.247) for the "Beltdown" flaw in Claude Code .

73
00:10:27,445 --> 00:10:47,520
Root cause: Claude's harness executed certain Git commands, such as , outside the macOS Seatbelt sandbox and unhardened; a poisoned, nested  could trigger a file-index refresh via skill auto-loading and thereby bypass the sandbox boundary – the Seatbelt rule against renaming nested  folders was simply missing .

74
00:10:47,520 --> 00:10:54,307
"If these frontier models are so good, how come they're not finding these critical vulnerabilities in their own products?"

75
00:10:54,307 --> 00:10:56,907
— Or Hiltch, CTO, Accomplish

76
00:10:56,907 --> 00:11:09,607
What we learn: Sandbox boundaries must apply even to "harmless" helper commands like Git operations – every exception made "outside the sandbox because it's just a read command" is a potential escape path.

77
00:11:09,607 --> 00:11:10,745
Number of the Week.

78
00:11:10,745 --> 00:11:12,207
26 seconds

79
00:11:12,207 --> 00:11:22,932
That's how long the PaperCut attacker's AI agent swarm took, according to GreyNoise's analysis, to compromise at least 11 organizations once the full campaign was underway .

80
00:11:22,932 --> 00:11:36,182
Compared to classic manual attack campaigns, which often take hours to days to reach a comparable number of compromises, this figure shows just how radically AI orchestration is reshaping the speed of mass attacks.

